User POV: two audiences. First, developers running git-ai from the CLI (git-ai commit / pr /
setup). What they perceive as the product is the generated commit & PR text, the commands and
flags, the setup wizard, and which providers/models work. Second, developers who install the
waxmard-git-ai Python package and import git_ai directly, never touching the CLI: for them
the product is the surface in __init__.py's __all__ — the signatures, what each argument
means, and what the functions return. "User-facing" means a change to either of those.

This repo writes scopeless Conventional Commits — do not add a scope.

Type rules from this POV:
- A new command, flag, provider, or setup capability → feat
- Editing a prompt (python/git_ai/prompts/*.txt) or a classification rule so the generated
  commit/PR output changes → feat or fix. The prompts ARE product behavior, never docs/chore.
- Changing what an exported function accepts, returns, or assumes about its inputs → feat or
  fix, never refactor — including when the diff only touches prompt prose or python/ internals
- Restructuring lib/ or python/ internals with no change to the CLI, the generated output, or
  the git_ai public API → refactor
- BATS or pytest changes only → test
- shellcheck, lefthook, line-limit, release tooling, or CI workflows → ci or build
- docs/src templates and the generated guides (README/AGENTS/CLAUDE/CONTRIBUTING) → docs

Breaking (!) from this POV: removing or renaming an __all__ export, or changing what an
argument existing callers already pass is taken to mean. A signature that still accepts the
old call but interprets it differently is the case most easily missed — it is breaking, and
the CLI staying identical does not make it otherwise.
